From e3d76f9b57e7a8bd4ab4411a9958027fe7ec3cdf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ole Algoritme <olealgoritme@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 14:59:03 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] nak: a register copy of an immediate serves the next uses in
 its block (NAK_IMM_CSE)

An FFMA by two constants (x * 34.0 + 1.0, say) needs one of them in a
register, and the legalizer made a new copy for every such instruction:
the biggest Steel Nomad compute shader (2a040240) had 1461 copies of 11
distinct immediates, 632 of them of 1.0.  The reference compiler's code
for the same shader has 71.

Like the uniform-value copies (NAK_DEBUG=noucse), a copy now serves the
later uses of the same immediate in its block, as long as the previous
use is at most 16 instructions back: a constant held across a long
stretch costs the scheduler a register there.

Even so, in fragment and vertex shaders the longer live ranges cost
occupancy on some shaders (34 of 4245 CS2 fragment shaders lose warps),
so by default this is for compute shaders only, where no shader in the
Steel Nomad and CS2 corpora changed its register count class.
NAK_IMM_CSE=1 turns it on for every stage, 0 off.

knobs.rs collects the knobs of this series; they go into
nak_gpr_cap_key() (bits 25 and up) and so into the shader cache keys.
NVK's check of that key now allows all 32 bits (the key sits in bits
16-47 of the compiler flags), and the NVK_SHADER_STATS header prints the
register caps only when one is set.

Offline, GB202: Steel Nomad compute 70416 -> 69410 instructions,
2a040240 20091 -> 19123; CS2 compute -0.4%.
